# `kept` is allocated with storage for doubles because a value chosen among the numbers that the iteration computes is stored into it. That is a speculation.
# In an array of doubles a NaN marks a hole, so the specialized version stores a number raw only after it has passed `x === x`. A number that fails the test restarts the iteration as written,
# and the store as written converts the storage to one for boxed values. That test alone stands between a NaN and a hole here.
# Fault injection, testing=AssumeNoNaNStores: the test is omitted. In a default build the element would silently disappear.
# With the switches that make the emitters check proofs, the raw store is checked.
# RUN(TAKEN): walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# TAKEN: NewArray {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# TAKEN: isLoopHead=true
# TAKEN: StrictEq [[stored]], [[stored]] form=Numbers
# TAKEN: ExtendArrayLength
# TAKEN-NEXT: PutDoubleIndex
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all globalThis.fails = "yes";
# RIGHT: true 13 7,2,3,NaN,5,6,7,8,9,10,11,12,13
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(PASSES): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime,AssumeNoNaNStores
